<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">company</ansi>

Your company is you, the leader, plus up to 4 companions who fight and
travel beside you. They stand with you in formation
(<ansi fg="command">help formation</ansi>), share your chemistry in battle
(<ansi fg="command">help chemistry</ansi>), and need food and rest just like
you do (<ansi fg="command">help survival</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help camp</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Finding companions</ansi>

Settlements have recruiters: a person or a posted notice offering
candidates for hire. <ansi fg="command">look</ansi> in a recruiter's room shows the
notice and the candidates you can still take, with their price: "free"
for a once-only offer, or "won't join you" for one your company would
refuse (see Alignment, below). Anyone already in your company, and a free
offer you've already taken, isn't listed; a full company is noted.
<ansi fg="command">look post</ansi> (or the notice's name) reads the notice again.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Your own recruits</ansi>

Settlement recruiters (the Waymark Inn's hiring slate, the Trappers'
Post) also post recruits of your own: 3 at a time as the world ships,
listed after the regulars. Each is a new face with their own name, archetype, level (within
one of yours), alignment, price, and a line about them. Your list is yours
alone: another player at the same notice sees different people, and what
you hire never changes their list, or theirs yours.

The list changes over time. Each recruit waits a while (half a game day
to two game days, as the world ships), then moves on and someone new takes their place, one
face at a time; time you spend away counts. Hire one and their place
stays empty for a little while before someone new turns up. One your
company would refuse is marked "won't join you", the same as any other
candidate. At the recruiter, type a recruit's first name to hire, look
at, or inspect them (<ansi fg="command">company recruit hild</ansi>). Once hired, they keep their
own name in your company, even if two of them are the same archetype.

  <ansi fg="command">look [candidate]</ansi>
  Read a candidate's description on the notice.

  <ansi fg="command">company recruit</ansi>
  List who is hiring here, with their archetype, level, alignment, gear,
  and price.

  <ansi fg="command">company recruit [candidate]</ansi>
  Take one on. Most candidates cost gold; some are offered free, but only
  once ever, per account. If your company won't have them (see
  Alignment, below), they say so instead of joining.

  <ansi fg="command">company inspect [candidate]</ansi>
  Check a candidate's alignment against your company's before you pay:
  whether they would join, and whether they would join uneasily.

<ansi fg="command">company summon [name]</ansi> enlists a companion directly,
without a recruiter, but only the few the world allows (a training dummy,
as the world ships).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Moving together</ansi>

Your companions follow you from room to room. You aren't told each one
arriving; the rooms you leave and enter hear one line for the whole
company ("Dain leads their company towards the east exit.", "Dain arrives
from the west, their company behind."). Any other move (a companion on
its own, or following you after a flight, a journey, or a sneak) still
shows its own line. <ansi fg="command">look</ansi> lists your companions by name,
without the ♥friend tag other charmed creatures carry; in a party of
players, <ansi fg="command">party</ansi> lists them as Company.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Your roster</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">company status</ansi>
  Every companion's number, name, level, archetype, alignment, loyalty,
  and whether they're present, awaiting restoration, fallen, or lost for
  good.

  <ansi fg="command">company gear [member]</ansi>
  What a companion is wearing and carrying.

  <ansi fg="command">company dismiss [member]</ansi>
  Let a companion go. <ansi fg="command">company dismiss all</ansi> lets your
  whole company go.

A fallen companion stays on your roster, waiting to be raised at a church
or a village shaman (<ansi fg="command">help resurrect</ansi>). Wait too long
and they're lost for good; <ansi fg="command">company status</ansi> shows
anyone lost.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Archetype</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">company archetype [member] [archetype]</ansi>
  Give a companion without one their archetype. This is permanent, the
  same as your own choice (<ansi fg="command">help archetype</ansi>). It sets
  what they can be trained to do and which utility skills they can use
  for the company (<ansi fg="command">help autoskill</ansi>,
  <ansi fg="command">help trap</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Alignment and chemistry</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">company alignment</ansi>
  Your company's alignment: yours, each companion's, and the average.
  See <ansi fg="command">help alignment</ansi> for what it means and how it
  drifts.

  <ansi fg="command">company chemistry</ansi>
  How well your company fights together after time served side by side.
  See <ansi fg="command">help chemistry</ansi>.

See also: <ansi fg="command">help formation</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help alignment</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help chemistry</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help archetype</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help standing</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help survival</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help resurrect</ansi>.